The RTPIP program transfers files between OS/8 devices and a RT-11 floppy
disk.  Either single- or double-density disks may be used with the appro-
priate disk drive.  Commands are similar to those used in OS/8 PIP:  '/Z'
means 'zero the directory', '/L' means 'list the directory', and '/I' in-
dicates 'image mode'.  The default transfer mode is ASCII, using the pro-
per parity and EOF mark for each system; the creation date of the file is
also preserved.  'Image mode' allows non-ASCII files (e.g. SV, BN, etc.),
to be archived along with the source files.  Since a double-density RT-11
disk holds the equivalent of 1235 OS/8 blocks, RTPIP offers an economical
way to store files of permanent interest.

For more information,  please contact  Jim van Zee  at  Lab Data Systems,
10320 Ravenna Avenue N.E.  Seattle,  Washington  98125.
